<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spiro Mounds</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 800–<a href="/years/1450">1450</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Spiro Mounds is an archaeological site on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkansas">Arkansas</a> River in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>, a centre of the Caddoan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture</a> from about 800 to 1450. Craig Mound, one of its twelve earthen mounds, held a hollow burial chamber, the Great Mortuary, that preserved textiles, feathers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and engraved conch shell cups and gorgets. Most people moved to nearby villages around 1250, but Spiro remained a ceremonial centre and burial ground. Treasure hunters who tunnelled into Craig Mound in the 1930s sold its contents to collectors, prompting a state law protecting the site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spiro-mounds.jpg" alt="Spiro Mounds" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Herb Roe, www.chromesun.com · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spiro_Big_Boy_effigy_pipe_HRoe_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The site covers about 150 acres of floodplain on the southern side of the Arkansas River; the modern town of Spiro grew up about seven miles to the south. Of its 12 mounds, built layer by layer from basketfuls of earth, one is a burial mound, two are temple mounds and nine are house mounds. Most surround a planned and levelled oval plaza where religious rituals and the game of chunkey took place, and the largest platform mound, Brown Mound, had an earthen ramp to its summit. More than twenty related villages lay within five miles of the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>People were active at Spiro for at least 8,000 years. Under the Arkansas Valley Caddoan culture it became the seat of a regional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdom</a> and one of four regional centres of the Caddoan Mississippian world, with Cahokia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moundville-archaeological-site">Moundville</a> and Etowah. Spiro took part in the Southeastern Ceremonial Complex and seems to have served as a gateway town, passing resources from the Great Plains to the centres farther east. Its traders acquired coloured flint from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a>, conch shells from the Gulf Coast and mica from the Carolinas, and it was apparently the only centre to obtain obsidian from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachuca">Pachuca</a>. Some of its finest stone effigy pipes came from Cahokia.</p><p>Most of the mounds were built in the Harlan Phase (1050–1250 CE). Archaeologists link the move of the population to nearby villages to less predictable seasonal rains at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-ice-age">Little Ice Age</a>. Spiro was at the peak of its importance in the 13th and 14th centuries, its ceremonial and mortuary use seeming to grow after most people had left, and nearby communities lasted until 1600.</p><p>In 1936 five prospectors leased the mounds for $300 and formed the Pocola Mining Company. They discarded human remains, textiles, basketry and feathers, destroyed about one-third of the artifacts in Craig Mound, and finally dynamited the burial chamber. Archaeologists from the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>, funded by the Works Progress Administration, excavated parts of the site between 1936 and 1941.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Oklahoma Historical Society opened the Spiro Mounds Archaeological Center at Fort Coffee in 1978. The site is listed on the National Register of Historic Places and is Oklahoma's only Archeological State Park. The Caddo Nation of Oklahoma and the Wichita and Affiliated Tribes are recognised as cultural descendants of its builders.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Spiro Mounds” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its products and ideas travelled widely: flint-clay figurines and pipes carved in the St Louis area were buried in the Craig Mound at <b>Spiro</b> in present-day Oklahoma, and Mississippian towns as far north as Aztalan in present-day Wisconsin show close ties to the American Bottom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cahokia">Cahokia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Etowah in Georgia, occupied from about 1000 to 1550, had three main platform mounds, the largest 19 metres high, behind a palisade and ditch, and <b>Spiro</b>, on the Arkansas River in eastern Oklahoma, was a centre of the Caddoan Mississippian world from about 800 to 1450.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture · Society</a></li></ul></section>
